{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "artifact": "data/outputs/aus_agent_v2/20260806T224918111210+1000.i_m_currently_working_on.output.json", "observable_system": "You are the observable-completeness scout for a research system. You receive\nthe original request, the primary plan, and a semantic obligation inventory\nfrom another independent scout. Find only material, independently scoreable\nanswer units that those inputs still leave implicit. Do not answer the request,\nstate facts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nWork at sentence granularity. A proposed check must be satisfiable by one\ncompact sentence or clause and must have a clear yes/no test in the finished\nanswer. Prefer checks involving an explicit count, literal name, worked link,\ncontrast, scope qualifier, or repeated per-part requirement. Do not return a\nbroad instruction such as \"be comprehensive\", \"give more detail\", or \"consider\nlimitations\". Do not repeat an existing check in narrower wording.\n\nUse the request's answer archetype as a recall aid:\n- For a novice research guide, test for expanded acronyms, named usable\n  software, concrete structured and unstructured input examples, one complete\n  record-to-result walkthrough, alternative analysis methods, named governing\n  mechanisms, enough domain examples to make the study real, and the requested\n  task order.\n- For a society-wide judgment, test for the requested number of domains and a\n  positive, negative, mechanism, and evidence unit in each; named platforms or\n  cases from more than one relevant region; a justified time/geography and\n  counterfactual; short- versus long-run effects; demographic heterogeneity;\n  major technology transitions such as mobile access, ranking, or feedback;\n  and a direct contrast between relevant regulatory environments.\n- For a board strategy, test for named owners, measurable product edge,\n  economics and capital gates, government programs, risk triggers, milestones,\n  and long-term strategic options.\n- For a proof or possibility answer, test for the exact conclusion, named\n  definitions and limits, one end-to-end derivation or counterexample, standard\n  dynamics, resource assumptions, and the boundary between conditional and\n  universal claims.\n- For a tutorial or series, test the requested part count and ensure every part\n  gets its own definition, worked example, variation or alternative, pitfall,\n  takeaway, and explicit link to earlier parts.\n\nThese prompts are not a checklist to copy. Select only checks material to the\nactual request. When a named term or example must appear literally, put it in\n`must_mention`; otherwise use an empty list. Search phrases are not obligations.\nDo not lower a count or relax the request. Rank by expected evaluation value\nper answer word.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"term|evidence|mechanism|comparison|scope|safety|format|penalty\",\"requirement\":\"one atomic observable requirement\",\"must_mention\":[\"exact term\"],\"reason\":\"why the current inventories can miss it\",\"search_leads\":[\"one compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most six additions. Keep each requirement under 28 words, each reason\nunder 30 words, at most two exact terms, and at most one search lead. Do not\ncombine independent checks with a list joined by \"and\". Do not wrap the JSON\nin Markdown.\n", "observable_request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.\n\nPRIMARY PLAN\n\n1. **DELIVERABLE:** Produce a seven-part beginner-oriented guide: interpret the assignment; show two synthetic EHR examples; survey NLP methods; identify and map CDC WONDER Asian subgroup categories; design prevalence analyses for at least two autoimmune diseases; list skills; and provide a phased research protocol.\n\n2. **AUDIENCE:** Assume familiarity with basic programming and statistics but no knowledge of clinical informatics, NLP pipelines, epidemiologic prevalence, or CDC WONDER. Progress from data structure and terminology to extraction, validation, subgroup mapping, and prevalence estimation.\n\n3. **EXPLICIT:** Explain what de-identified EHR data may contain, what “extract” and “abstract the same subgroups” probably mean, which NLP techniques could apply, how subgroup-specific autoimmune prevalence could be estimated, and what the student should learn and do next. Clearly mark interpretations that require confirmation from the professor rather than presenting them as settled facts.\n\n4. **DEFINITION:** Plainly define EHR, de-identification, structured versus unstructured data, NLP, named-entity recognition, ontology, phenotype/case definition, race versus ethnicity versus ancestry, denominator, prevalence, confidence interval, validation set, precision, recall, and F1 score.\n\n5. **EXAMPLE:** Include at least two fictional, non-identifiable EHR excerpts—such as a demographics field and a clinical/social-history note—and demonstrate candidate subgroup signals, negation, ambiguity, family references, and conflicting evidence. Also walk through at least two diseases with contrasting ascertainment challenges, without assuming that diagnosis codes alone establish disease.\n\n6. **IMPLIED:** Compare structured-field lookup, dictionaries/rules, fuzzy matching, clinical NER, supervised classifiers, weak supervision, and carefully governed LLM use; explain when NLP is unnecessary. Cover ICD/SNOMED-based phenotyping, medications and labs, missingness, class imbalance, multilabel identities, temporal changes, and manual chart-review adjudication.\n\n7. **EVIDENCE:** Search the current CDC WONDER dataset documentation, category labels, coding years, suppression rules, denominator availability, and API/export options; determine which specific WONDER database the professor means because subgroup granularity varies by dataset and year. Verify whether EHR labels can be crosswalked exactly or require an “unknown/other/multiple/ambiguous” policy.\n\n8. **EVIDENCE:** Locate at least one prior peer-reviewed EHR study extracting granular Asian ethnicity and report its dataset, method, and validation results, plus at least one concrete autoimmune-prevalence study or public dataset result establishing context. Also retrieve validated phenotype algorithms and reporting guidance for race/ethnicity and observational EHR studies.\n\n9. **IMPLIED:** Specify a defensible protocol: research question, cohort and observation window, inclusion criteria, disease case definitions, subgroup ontology, annotation guide, train/validation/test split, inter-annotator agreement, error analysis, denominator construction, age/sex standardization, uncertainty intervals, and sensitivity analyses.\n\n10. **SAFETY:** Address IRB/data-use approval, HIPAA-safe computing, minimum-necessary access, re-identification risk, small-cell suppression, stigmatizing interpretations, subgroup misclassification, selection and documentation bias, and inequitable model error. Qualify generalization by health system, geography, age, immigration/language context, calendar period, and care access; do not infer causation from prevalence differences.\n\n11. **BUDGET:** Allocate at most 930 words: assignment interpretation 100; EHR examples/definitions 140; NLP options 150; WONDER mapping 125; disease-prevalence design 180; validation/ethics 115; skills and phased plan 120. End each part with a takeaway, and finish with professor-clarification questions and immediate next actions.\n\nSEMANTIC OBLIGATIONS ALREADY FOUND\n\n[{\"kind\": \"term\", \"requirement\": \"Identify the exact CDC WONDER dataset, table fields, years, and available Asian subgroup categories before proposing label harmonization.\", \"must_mention\": [\"CDC WONDER\"], \"reason\": \"Race detail varies by WONDER dataset, year, and underlying death-certificate or population data; generic category claims may be wrong.\", \"search_leads\": [\"site:wonder.cdc.gov Asian detailed race categories dataset\"]}, {\"kind\": \"safety\", \"requirement\": \"Explain which de-identification pathway and institutional approvals govern access to narrative EHR data.\", \"must_mention\": [\"HIPAA Privacy Rule\", \"Safe Harbor\", \"Expert Determination\"], \"reason\": \"De-identified clinical text can retain identifiers, and research use may still require data-use controls, security review, or IRB determination.\", \"search_leads\": [\"HHS HIPAA de-identification Safe Harbor Expert Determination clinical text\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Show one concrete EHR example distinguishing structured demographics and diagnosis codes from unstructured notes containing ethnicity mentions.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Electronic Health Record (EHR)\"], \"reason\": \"The extraction task, available evidence, and suitable methods differ fundamentally between structured fields and free text.\", \"search_leads\": [\"EHR structured data unstructured clinical notes example\"]}, {\"kind\": \"comparison\", \"requirement\": \"Compare a rule-based lexicon baseline with supervised clinical-text classification, including annotation needs and evaluation metrics.\", \"must_mention\": [\"Named Entity Recognition (NER)\", \"precision\", \"recall\"], \"reason\": \"A useful plan must connect candidate NLP methods to labels, training resources, and measurable extraction performance rather than merely name algorithms.\", \"search_leads\": [\"clinical NLP ethnicity extraction NER precision recall\"]}, {\"kind\": \"mechanism\", \"requirement\": \"Define a validated autoimmune-disease phenotype and distinguish period prevalence from incidence using an eligible-population denominator.\", \"must_mention\": [\"period prevalence\", \"incidence\"], \"reason\": \"Counting diagnosis mentions is not prevalence; disease ascertainment, observation windows, repeated records, and denominators determine the estimate.\", \"search_leads\": [\"EHR computable phenotype autoimmune disease prevalence denominator\"]}, {\"kind\": \"evidence\", \"requirement\": \"Cite at least one prior study measuring autoimmune disease prevalence across specific Asian subgroups, not separate studies of ethnicity and autoimmune disease.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Direct precedent is needed to justify subgroup definitions, disease choices, expected disparities, and plausible analytic design.\", \"search_leads\": [\"Asian ethnic subgroups autoimmune disease prevalence study\"]}, {\"kind\": \"penalty\", \"requirement\": \"Avoid treating proxy-inferred ethnicity or an aggregate Asian category as representative of self-identified, heterogeneous Asian subgroups.\", \"must_mention\": [], \"reason\": \"Surname, language, birthplace, and note-derived labels can systematically misclassify people and conceal subgroup-specific prevalence differences.\", \"search_leads\": [\"EHR Asian subgroup ethnicity misclassification surname proxy bias\"]}, {\"kind\": \"format\", \"requirement\": \"Provide a staged workflow from data dictionary review through annotation, baseline modeling, validation, harmonization, prevalence estimation, and sensitivity analysis.\", \"must_mention